 ## Unreleased
+
+- **Error-watch ignores standalone Python exception-chain separators and groups connector causes independently of address order** (V-1189; `app/error_watch.py`). Both standard chaining lines are noise only as complete records, including registered-fix observation; added exception text stays actionable. Complete errno entries inside `ClientConnectorError`'s `Multiple exceptions` list are sorted without splitting address tuples or dropping repeated causes. Real connection failures still reach the alert threshold, while other exception classes, hosts, SSL modes and failure text retain separate signatures. Legacy connector alert keys are canonicalized on scan and old fix keys still match recurrences without losing their metadata. Synthetic offline checks cover actual aiohttp/Python exception formatting, boundaries, deduplication and state reload; the source-only worker lacks application dependencies for the full-checkout gate. No live data or service restart.
 
 - **Daily hub-idle watch applies to every orchestrator** (V-1184; `scripts/hub_idle_watch.py`). It infers an idle anchor from known state transitions instead of treating every later status log as a state change: `turn ended`, `waiting for bg jobs`, manual stop, and server shutdown have explicit handling; context-unknown and auto-report-skipped records preserve the prior state. Unrecognized events invalidate the inferred anchor. A candidate must currently be idle, use a `hub-` model, have a known transition strictly older than 24 hours, and resolve to its nearest owning orchestrator. Workers with no logged transition are omitted because `created_at` precedes their actual idle start; full coverage needs a persisted idle-start timestamp. Empty results print nothing; missing, malformed, or empty databases return `HUB_IDLE_ERROR`. The existing daily job remains unchanged until post-merge activation; merge and kill remain subject to acceptance and `worker_wip`/kill gate.
 
